00:03:10.421 --> 00:03:13.661
Caroline: Hello everyone,
and welcome to my session.

00:03:14.012 --> 00:03:19.296
I'm really excited to be with all of you
here today at WordPress Accessibility Day.

00:03:19.861 --> 00:03:25.660
This session is titled Embedded Alt Text:
Smarter Image Workflows in WordPress

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:31.529
7.0, Moving From Blank Slates to
Scalable, Portable Image Accessibility.

00:03:32.363 --> 00:03:38.005
For decades, image accessibility has
suffered from a fundamental workflow flaw.

00:03:38.325 --> 00:03:43.815
Alternative text or alt text is treated
as an afterthought, hastily typed into

00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:46.341
a box right before hitting publish.

00:03:46.743 --> 00:03:51.483
And I know what some of you are thinking
out there, how are we still stuck in

00:03:51.484 --> 00:03:53.749
the same place after all these years?

00:03:54.138 --> 00:03:56.332
And that's a really g- great question.

00:03:56.347 --> 00:04:00.529
I find myself asking myself
that, um, all the time.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.945
But my honest answer, it's
because alt text is really a

00:04:04.947 --> 00:04:08.057
lot harder than it seems, truly.

00:04:08.387 --> 00:04:13.060
Uh, on the content side, alt text
is a technical writing skill.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:17.017
Um, you're translating visual
intent into user experience.

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:22.304
On the workflow side, most platforms
simply weren't built for accessibility

00:04:22.322 --> 00:04:26.243
at scale, and they assumed that
adding alt text at the very end

00:04:26.243 --> 00:04:28.312
of the process would be enough.

00:04:28.607 --> 00:04:29.885
Turns out, it isn't.

00:04:30.222 --> 00:04:36.533
Alt text touches photographers, writers,
DAMs, CMSs, and it's really across

00:04:36.534 --> 00:04:41.704
the workflow, so actually connecting
those dots, um, across platforms as

00:04:41.705 --> 00:04:46.650
well is really what's missing and
creating this massive operational

00:04:46.662 --> 00:04:48.911
hurdle for organizations to manage.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.477
Um, so no surprise we're still here today.

00:04:51.794 --> 00:04:56.530
Um, but we're not gonna focus on best
practices around writing alt text.

00:04:56.811 --> 00:05:00.813
Uh, we're actually going to cover
the other side of alt text that you

00:05:00.813 --> 00:05:05.219
rarely hear about, and that's the
technical and production side that

00:05:05.219 --> 00:05:09.682
really acts as the silent killer
for your accessibility initiatives.

00:05:09.899 --> 00:05:11.582
Um, it's all workflow.

00:05:11.784 --> 00:05:15.712
Um, that's what we're-- where we're
seeing, uh, these issues arise and

00:05:15.777 --> 00:05:17.357
alt text fall through the cracks.

00:05:17.693 --> 00:05:21.647
So in this presentation, I'm
going to break down these systemic

00:05:21.659 --> 00:05:23.174
issues that are taking place.

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:28.565
We're gonna look at how embedded alt
text in image files works, uh, under the

00:05:28.566 --> 00:05:33.257
hood and behind the scenes, and we're
going to see how that embedded alt text

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:38.928
creates portable, scalable workflows
that save hours of manual effort.

00:05:39.649 --> 00:05:42.346
Also, we're going to
run through a live demo.

00:05:42.362 --> 00:05:43.082
Very exciting.

00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:48.115
I'm gonna show you how to s- how this,
uh, new update to WordPress 7.0 with

00:05:48.115 --> 00:05:52.535
embedded alt text actually works,
um, so we can see it all in action.

00:05:52.993 --> 00:05:54.135
All right, let's dive in.

00:05:55.525 --> 00:06:00.008
So before we look into this WordPress
update, we need to talk about the

00:06:00.056 --> 00:06:05.574
underlying engine that makes it possible,
and that is IPTC photo metadata.

00:06:05.911 --> 00:06:09.763
So for anyone unfamiliar
with the term photo metadata,

00:06:10.118 --> 00:06:12.262
it's simply data about data.

00:06:12.586 --> 00:06:16.560
Every time you take a picture on
a phone or a camera, there are

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.041
technical specs like the resolution,
the date, uh, the camera model.

00:06:22.317 --> 00:06:27.002
All of that information is automatically
embedded into the image file.

00:06:27.445 --> 00:06:31.898
Beyond these technical specs, we
also have descriptive information

00:06:32.112 --> 00:06:37.099
like captions, keywords, copyright,
and creator, um, credits.

00:06:37.348 --> 00:06:43.878
So this is where IPTC comes in, and
IPTC stands for the International

00:06:43.919 --> 00:06:46.363
Press and Telecommunications Council.

00:06:46.841 --> 00:06:50.782
They are a global standards body
that defines photo metadata fields

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:56.071
for news agencies, publishers,
museums, and software tools worldwide.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:01.847
Having this single worldwide standard
is absolutely vital because it

00:07:01.847 --> 00:07:06.493
ensures metadata is written in one--
that's written in one professional

00:07:06.493 --> 00:07:11.102
tool can actually be read by another
that also supports that standard.

00:07:11.307 --> 00:07:16.438
So it allows us to connect systems and
collaborate seamlessly across them.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:21.307
And, uh, this is also a,
a personal note from me.

00:07:21.355 --> 00:07:26.961
Uh, I was part of the team that helped get
accessibility added to the IPTC standard.

00:07:26.973 --> 00:07:34.114
So I joined, um, IPTC in May 2021,
working alongside the Photo Metadata

00:07:34.123 --> 00:07:38.403
Working Group and also industry partners
like the Smithsonian Institution,

00:07:38.631 --> 00:07:43.452
who were actively pushing for these
very same accessibility fields, just

00:07:43.457 --> 00:07:44.968
coincidentally at the same time.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.589
So, um, the stars aligned, and we
all got to work on adding these

00:07:48.592 --> 00:07:51.618
accessibility fields to the IPTC standard.

00:07:51.966 --> 00:07:57.154
Um, and that hard work paid off just
a few months later, um, in October

00:07:57.176 --> 00:08:03.139
2021, when the IPTC announced a truly
game-changing milestone for digital

00:08:03.141 --> 00:08:07.986
inclusion, and they officially updated
their photo metadata standard to include

00:08:08.151 --> 00:08:13.484
two metadata ex- um, accessibility
properties: alt text accessibility and

00:08:13.485 --> 00:08:15.278
extended description accessibility.

00:08:15.573 --> 00:08:20.243
So for the very first time, um,
alternative text, um, is not trapped

00:08:20.243 --> 00:08:22.957
inside an isolated website database.

00:08:23.221 --> 00:08:27.225
It all of a sudden had the possibility
of becoming portable, um, a

00:08:27.225 --> 00:08:32.671
standardized property embedded directly
inside the image, uh, file itself.

00:08:32.975 --> 00:08:35.165
Um, so very exciting milestone.

00:08:35.448 --> 00:08:39.153
And ever since then, it's been an
advocacy effort to get, um, more and

00:08:39.153 --> 00:08:43.542
more software providers supporting
the accessibility properties

00:08:46.872 --> 00:08:49.900
So, what is so great about this update?

00:08:50.372 --> 00:08:55.440
To put it simply, WordPress 7.0
automatically pulls this embedded

00:08:55.454 --> 00:09:00.154
image description data into media
uploads, and this eliminates

00:09:00.156 --> 00:09:02.424
the need for manual data entry.

00:09:02.925 --> 00:09:07.815
So this update, um, just including
some specifics here on the slide, um,

00:09:07.848 --> 00:09:14.526
it's, it's landed thanks to Changeset
61841 on WordPress Core, and I have

00:09:14.528 --> 00:09:18.610
a little blurb here that explains
it, and it reads, "This Changeset

00:09:18.645 --> 00:09:27.060
introduces a new wp_get_image_alttext
function that extracts image alt text

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:29.749
metadata from image IPTC metadata."

00:09:30.029 --> 00:09:32.926
So this happened on, in May 2026.

00:09:33.276 --> 00:09:34.579
Um, very exciting.

00:09:35.086 --> 00:09:37.582
Why is this specific
function such a big deal?

00:09:38.334 --> 00:09:43.326
Most platforms actually strip out this
embedded metadata when the image is

00:09:43.358 --> 00:09:48.665
uploaded, which really causes issues
for connecting the dots and creates

00:09:48.665 --> 00:09:53.058
this kind of useless duplication
of effort, um, when alt text or

00:09:53.058 --> 00:09:55.355
really any metadata already exists.

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.429
Um, you actually have to find
a, a workaround, or you have

00:09:58.430 --> 00:09:59.759
to populate it all again.

00:10:00.102 --> 00:10:05.620
So overcoming that hurdle, um, really
sets a new standard for streamlining

00:10:05.637 --> 00:10:08.978
alt text production, where we can
now start to have a conversation

00:10:09.255 --> 00:10:15.032
about how we can manage images as
they change hands across a workflow.

00:10:15.774 --> 00:10:21.237
So with this, um, this update, the alt
text field is already populated within

00:10:21.237 --> 00:10:26.328
the file's built-in description the
very moment it hits the media library.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.689
So we're gonna see that more
in the, in the demo later.

00:10:28.992 --> 00:10:32.899
Um, but from there, uh, content
creators simply need to review what

00:10:32.901 --> 00:10:37.611
that baseline description is, and
then make any necessary tweaks for

00:10:37.612 --> 00:10:39.983
context, and then finally hit publish.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.146
But that information is preserved.

00:10:42.322 --> 00:10:44.137
Uh, they don't have to start from scratch.

00:10:44.322 --> 00:10:48.920
Significantly easer, easier than
just staring at a blank box and

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:50.451
writing something, I promise you.

00:10:51.608 --> 00:10:56.532
So, to see how passing descriptive
metadata works in practice, um, I wanna

00:10:56.541 --> 00:10:58.592
think about a real-world scenario.

00:10:58.606 --> 00:11:00.631
So just kind of follow along with me.

00:11:01.214 --> 00:11:06.722
Think about, um, you're a, a web
designer, um, and you're, you're tasked

00:11:06.722 --> 00:11:11.413
with creating this About Us page that
pic- that has all of the different team

00:11:11.428 --> 00:11:17.428
members' images, um, on it, and you need
to make that page accessible, right?

00:11:17.781 --> 00:11:22.315
So, let's go back to the very first
time these pictures were captured.

00:11:22.505 --> 00:11:24.807
We have a company shoot day.

00:11:25.045 --> 00:11:29.954
A photographer is taking headshots of
30 team members that are present, and

00:11:29.955 --> 00:11:35.227
then a week later, those images land on
the desk of you, um, the, the individual

00:11:35.232 --> 00:11:39.607
who's supposed to add these to the
webpage and then make the website go live.

00:11:40.021 --> 00:11:47.280
Um, so when it comes to, um, writing alt
text, uh, you might find yourself as, you

00:11:47.281 --> 00:11:49.805
know, a step removed from that process.

00:11:50.061 --> 00:11:54.811
You're staring at 30 headshots, and
they're suddenly like, "How do I

00:11:54.823 --> 00:11:59.420
visually describe my colleagues in
a way that's respectful, accurate,

00:11:59.602 --> 00:12:02.355
helpful?" And it feels uncomfortable.

00:12:02.362 --> 00:12:05.358
It's time-consuming, and
it's easy to get wrong.

00:12:05.997 --> 00:12:08.737
So, that is something
that happens quite often.

00:12:09.013 --> 00:12:13.665
The images that were taken are a few
steps removed from the person actually

00:12:13.675 --> 00:12:17.836
publishing them, so they don't feel
empowered with the information that they

00:12:17.838 --> 00:12:21.113
need to actually write quality alt text.

00:12:21.286 --> 00:12:24.753
So they either end up skipping the
alt text or writing something generic.

00:12:25.101 --> 00:12:29.694
So when it comes to team members, um,
what's relevant is how they look, right?

00:12:29.945 --> 00:12:32.680
That's what people are curious about
when they go to an About Us page.

00:12:32.776 --> 00:12:36.194
But oftentimes we see kind of generic
labels with just their name or

00:12:36.195 --> 00:12:39.796
something like that, um, which leaves
a lot to desire, uh, be desired.

00:12:39.796 --> 00:12:42.855
We can't actually understand
what these individuals look like.

00:12:43.634 --> 00:12:48.391
So, imagine a different scenario
where we're utilizing IPTC metadata.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:52.752
At the shoot, while setting up the
shot, the photographer can simply ask

00:12:52.771 --> 00:12:56.409
the subject of the photo, "How would
you like to be visually described?" And

00:12:56.409 --> 00:13:00.718
that individual can provide their own
v- authentic self-description, their

00:13:00.719 --> 00:13:04.180
clothing, their hair, whatever they want
to talk about with their visual features.

00:13:04.658 --> 00:13:09.939
Uh, and then that helps at the editing
station because they can then take those

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.752
details, they can standardize them,
they can create consistency across the

00:13:13.752 --> 00:13:17.390
range of descriptions, um, and they
can essentially just polish them up.

00:13:17.742 --> 00:13:21.914
Um, so a lot of assistance provided
where we're, we're building on

00:13:21.916 --> 00:13:26.017
that baseline description that was
provided from that shoot day, and

00:13:26.074 --> 00:13:30.309
then we're bringing it into, um,
a polished state for the website.

00:13:31.086 --> 00:13:36.396
So, the idea is, we're not starting
from scratch, and, um, our colleague

00:13:36.397 --> 00:13:39.887
is being supported with information
they need to publish really

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:42.364
high-quality alt text on the website.

00:13:45.789 --> 00:13:50.250
To understand why this technical
update is so necessary, we need to

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:54.379
take a step back and look at the
real world data associated with this.

00:13:54.782 --> 00:14:00.067
So according to WebAIM Million's
report, 53% of the top 1 million

00:14:00.071 --> 00:14:02.249
homepages are missing alt text.

00:14:02.723 --> 00:14:07.854
And honestly, that 53% missing alt text
is really just the tip of the iceberg.

00:14:08.344 --> 00:14:13.265
The amount of unhelpful alt text,
bad alt text, generic alt te- text on

00:14:13.281 --> 00:14:19.727
homepages is much higher when you factor
in, um, you know, folks using image

00:14:19.732 --> 00:14:22.915
filenames or generic labels like graphic.

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:26.746
Um, over a third of homepage
images fail to give screen reader

00:14:26.746 --> 00:14:28.862
users any meaningful context.

00:14:28.867 --> 00:14:31.897
So, um, that's adding to this number.

00:14:32.639 --> 00:14:34.134
So why is this happening?

00:14:34.173 --> 00:14:38.321
Why hasn't that percentage moved
significantly in the past five years?

00:14:38.321 --> 00:14:42.269
We really haven't seen significant
improvement, and I don't think we

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:47.413
can blame a lack of awareness of alt
text anymore because it's one of the

00:14:47.414 --> 00:14:51.968
most common accessibility, uh, uh,
requirements that's, that's talked about.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:57.250
Um, for anyone working in content
publishing, it's more likely a broken

00:14:57.259 --> 00:15:02.099
workflow problem that manifests in three
distinct ways, which I have up here

00:15:02.101 --> 00:15:04.585
on the first slide, on, on this slide.

00:15:05.715 --> 00:15:08.495
The first is legacy archives.

00:15:08.586 --> 00:15:12.562
So this is retroactively fixing
thousands of images feels

00:15:12.563 --> 00:15:14.497
like an insurmountable chore.

00:15:14.737 --> 00:15:18.689
Opening every file one by one
stops teams before they begin.

00:15:19.045 --> 00:15:24.793
So when your asset library spans years
or decades, manual rem- remediation

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:28.275
is so overwhelming that organizations
don't even know where to start.

00:15:29.271 --> 00:15:31.911
The second is new content overhead.

00:15:32.458 --> 00:15:36.483
With over 40 images on the average
homepage starting from a blank slate

00:15:36.513 --> 00:15:41.034
at publication, this is creating
massive administrative friction.

00:15:41.252 --> 00:15:46.074
So new content that's added to a page,
um, stopping at the last minute to

00:15:46.074 --> 00:15:51.834
write 40 individual descriptions right
before hitting publish, um, it may-might

00:15:51.835 --> 00:15:55.164
be the case where the individual
just doesn't, doesn't have time or

00:15:55.165 --> 00:15:59.353
expertise, and they end up hitting
publish without that alt text included.

00:15:59.675 --> 00:16:04.098
Um, so the work gets pushed off, and
then the backlog grows even larger.

00:16:04.804 --> 00:16:06.731
And finally, lost production work.

00:16:06.988 --> 00:16:09.958
Photographers and agencies
already write great alt text

00:16:09.958 --> 00:16:11.600
in Lightroom or Photo Mechanic.

00:16:11.871 --> 00:16:15.181
Um, historically WordPress and
other, many other platforms

00:16:15.202 --> 00:16:17.075
ignored or stripped this away.

00:16:17.327 --> 00:16:22.434
So, even though we may have had alt
text, it was being stripped out, and

00:16:22.444 --> 00:16:26.589
we were having to do this, um, copy
and paste effort, manual workarounds,

00:16:26.929 --> 00:16:29.395
and, um, that's simply frustrating.

00:16:29.448 --> 00:16:30.924
It stops a workflow right there.

00:16:31.736 --> 00:16:36.016
So upstream creators are frequently
spending time writing descriptions in

00:16:36.016 --> 00:16:40.769
professional shoots, but clients rarely
request this work because they assume

00:16:40.944 --> 00:16:43.144
that the CMS will just wipe it out anyway.

00:16:43.427 --> 00:16:47.915
So up until now, all of that diligent
production work, um, was ultimately

00:16:47.915 --> 00:16:52.142
ignored at the upload stage and forced
to be written-- rewritten from scratch.

00:16:52.367 --> 00:16:56.490
So that's the, that's the problem
that we're encountering and, um,

00:16:56.896 --> 00:17:01.174
the exact bottleneck that the
WordPress 7.0 update eliminates.

00:17:01.952 --> 00:17:05.970
So, to see why passing descriptive
metadata is so powerful in the

00:17:05.972 --> 00:17:10.636
real world, I want to give you
another example, um, uh, this time

00:17:10.658 --> 00:17:12.964
for e-commerce product workflows.

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:17.319
So, picture an online fashion
retailer launching a new

00:17:17.331 --> 00:17:19.014
seasonal conne- collection.

00:17:19.502 --> 00:17:23.335
They have hundreds of products, and
each product has six different im-

00:17:23.377 --> 00:17:28.425
images, all of the different angles,
model shots, um, maybe an outfit shot.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.956
Where does the product information live?

00:17:31.341 --> 00:17:35.779
Usually, it lives upstream in a DAM
or product information system, a

00:17:35.781 --> 00:17:38.036
PIM, and managed by the product team.

00:17:38.656 --> 00:17:42.841
In a traditional workflow, once those
product photos are exported and upload

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:47.148
to-- uploaded to WordPress, the content
manager has to manually retype or

00:17:47.152 --> 00:17:52.252
copy-paste descriptions for dozens
of image, uh, variations one by one.

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:55.799
It's tedious, it's error-prone,
and it usually results in

00:17:55.799 --> 00:17:57.615
generic alt text, to be honest.

00:17:58.016 --> 00:18:01.450
Um, now picture the
embedded IPTC workflow.

00:18:01.768 --> 00:18:06.806
Uh, we can start upstream again, the
product team this time embedding rich

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:10.516
baseline descriptions directly into
the image files during production.

00:18:10.847 --> 00:18:15.129
They're specifying color, cut,
texture, details that are all much

00:18:15.145 --> 00:18:17.248
closer to them as the product team.

00:18:17.869 --> 00:18:21.575
The workflow then moves to portable
import, where those assets are

00:18:21.623 --> 00:18:27.037
uploaded to WordPress and the CMS
reads the IPTC metadata automatically.

00:18:27.571 --> 00:18:31.837
So this results in zero data
loss and a supported workflow.

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:35.860
The alt text arrives pre-populated,
verified, and complete.

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:40.862
Now, if those same product images are
then syndicated to wholesaler partner

00:18:40.868 --> 00:18:45.537
si- sites or distributed to affiliate
networks, um, that accessibility

00:18:45.543 --> 00:18:47.382
can also travel with the file.

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:53.066
So, uh, a provider can coordinate,
uh, an external metadata, um, feed

00:18:53.223 --> 00:18:57.076
that actually provides that image
da- image data or just simply passing

00:18:57.077 --> 00:18:59.542
their files to, um, their provider.

00:18:59.553 --> 00:19:05.030
So again, if, if we have more folks
using this standard, we can use image

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:07.476
file metadata to pass this information.

00:19:10.673 --> 00:19:15.000
So let's talk about connecting
the dots and take a closer look at

00:19:15.004 --> 00:19:17.105
how this all works under the hood.

00:19:17.905 --> 00:19:24.083
So essentially, a core custom XML
parser reads the XMP metadata headers

00:19:24.203 --> 00:19:27.121
embedded inside every uploaded image.

00:19:27.491 --> 00:19:29.312
And here is the three-step workflow.

00:19:29.734 --> 00:19:33.465
First, you drag and drop the image,
and it lands in the WordPress media

00:19:33.466 --> 00:19:35.558
library, just like any other upload.

00:19:35.973 --> 00:19:38.186
Next, the fields are detected.

00:19:38.447 --> 00:19:43.081
Alt text accessibility metadata,
metadata is read from the file.

00:19:43.966 --> 00:19:47.501
And lastly, the baseline
alt text is populated.

00:19:47.795 --> 00:19:51.348
WordPress instantly populates
the media library settings, and

00:19:51.348 --> 00:19:54.574
this gives content managers a
foundational description to work with.

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:59.303
So I want to take a moment to mention
the necessity of contextual review

00:19:59.305 --> 00:20:00.924
here because it's very important.

00:20:01.522 --> 00:20:04.803
This is-- this automation is a foundation.

00:20:04.812 --> 00:20:09.199
It's definitely not a replacement
for human oversight, so publishers

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:14.032
must still evaluate and adjust the
alt text to fit each page's context.

00:20:14.523 --> 00:20:18.675
While this saves immense time, it
doesn't eliminate human judgment.

00:20:19.019 --> 00:20:23.226
A photographer upstream couldn't possibly
know how their photo will be used three

00:20:23.227 --> 00:20:25.557
months from now in a specific article.

00:20:25.946 --> 00:20:29.846
So complete rewrites of alt
text are entirely appropriate if

00:20:29.847 --> 00:20:31.452
the page context calls for it.

00:20:31.951 --> 00:20:36.581
Accuracy and quality remain the ultimate
priorities when it comes to publishing

00:20:36.598 --> 00:20:41.574
alt text online because ultimately, we're
creating experience for screen reader

00:20:41.574 --> 00:20:45.273
users, for assistive technology users,
and they're depending on these fields.

00:20:45.504 --> 00:20:47.629
So if we need to make any
changes, then we should.

00:20:48.945 --> 00:20:52.923
So while this r- this really does,
um, bridge the gap between creators

00:20:52.923 --> 00:20:56.651
and publishers, it's also turning
accessibility into a shared and

00:20:56.651 --> 00:20:58.173
collaborative responsibility.

00:20:59.697 --> 00:21:05.083
So let's talk through the IPTC
accessibility fields, um, for, um,

00:21:05.299 --> 00:21:08.483
versus traditional metadata, just so
we can distinguish the difference.

00:21:09.526 --> 00:21:09.754
Oops.

00:21:12.248 --> 00:21:14.848
So first up, we have title and headline.

00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:19.165
These are really built for file
management and visual page layout.

00:21:19.182 --> 00:21:24.989
The headline is a brief publishable
synopsis of the image, so not

00:21:25.231 --> 00:21:27.623
really the same as alt text at all.

00:21:28.485 --> 00:21:32.620
The caption description is
more for the contextual facts.

00:21:32.632 --> 00:21:37.586
Think the who, what, where, where
credits, um, of the image that

00:21:37.586 --> 00:21:39.141
are usually visible to everyone.

00:21:39.145 --> 00:21:41.598
Sometimes they're in a
caption under the news photo.

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:45.423
Those facts about the image are
also not the same as alt text.

00:21:46.699 --> 00:21:51.504
Now, alt text accessibility captures
visual meaning and intent, an objective

00:21:51.504 --> 00:21:55.907
description written to convey equivalent
visual meaning to screen reader users.

00:21:56.296 --> 00:22:00.528
So, emphasis on the visual
description and also the word meaning.

00:22:00.752 --> 00:22:04.449
We need purpose and meaning infused
into these descriptions, and that

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:08.212
comes from understanding the contextual
experience of that page, so the

00:22:08.222 --> 00:22:10.244
alt text is written in context.

00:22:11.242 --> 00:22:15.040
The extended description is more for
structured content with heading and

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:18.723
lists, and this is more appropriate
for complex visual-- visuals like

00:22:18.739 --> 00:22:22.874
charts or infographics, um, that
assistive technology can navigate

00:22:22.901 --> 00:22:27.117
seamlessly when there are those headings
and lists, um, actually applied.

00:22:27.584 --> 00:22:31.490
So, um, just a quick note here that
extended description accessibility

00:22:31.648 --> 00:22:38.197
is imported, preserved, and passed by
WordPress, um, but it is not, um, included

00:22:38.229 --> 00:22:43.421
in, in, into importing into a field
called extended description accessibility.

00:22:43.785 --> 00:22:46.926
And that's because, um,
extended descriptions require

00:22:46.938 --> 00:22:48.053
additional development.

00:22:48.508 --> 00:22:52.772
Um, we're running short on time, so I'm
going to kind of breeze through this,

00:22:52.778 --> 00:22:56.396
but there are three different approaches
to adding extended descriptions.

00:22:56.641 --> 00:23:00.191
Um, search for WebAIM complex images.

00:23:00.356 --> 00:23:04.232
This will take you to a page where
there are-- there's guidance on how to

00:23:04.265 --> 00:23:06.513
add extended descriptions to websites.

00:23:06.848 --> 00:23:09.597
It's not the primary focus
of this presentation, so I'm

00:23:09.597 --> 00:23:10.746
just going to jump ahead.

00:23:11.105 --> 00:23:17.187
So beyond CMS databases, um,
embedded alt text really becomes

00:23:17.227 --> 00:23:19.186
a portable source of truth.

00:23:19.551 --> 00:23:25.266
So, first thing we need to
consider is ecosystem consistency.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:28.990
Embedded alt text travels
everywhere, enabling adaptation,

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:33.170
driving system-wide consistency,
and eliminating duplicate work.

00:23:33.951 --> 00:23:35.986
It also promotes shared accountability.

00:23:36.142 --> 00:23:40.294
It holds the platforms and ourselves to
real standards that matter for users,

00:23:40.525 --> 00:23:45.266
instead of focusing on flashy features
that honestly often break accessibility.

00:23:45.547 --> 00:23:46.769
Um, this is really important.

00:23:46.812 --> 00:23:47.715
We need to get this right.

00:23:48.212 --> 00:23:50.086
And finally, data in-integrity.

00:23:50.574 --> 00:23:53.522
Save the work, pass it
on, adapt as you go.

00:23:53.701 --> 00:23:56.998
It's a beautiful workflow that
preserves and passes quality

00:23:57.002 --> 00:23:59.612
metadata, um, that scales alt text.

