00:33:39.598 --> 00:33:44.238
So all four options below, um, all
manage alt text in a spreadsheet and

00:33:44.239 --> 00:33:46.729
then write it back into files at scale.

00:33:47.199 --> 00:33:49.534
So I'm gonna click through,
um, a few options that you

00:33:49.535 --> 00:33:51.319
have to, to update in bulk.

00:33:51.702 --> 00:33:56.386
The first is using Metadata Deluxe,
which is an extension of Adobe Bridge.

00:33:56.482 --> 00:33:58.286
Highly recommend checking this out.

00:33:58.593 --> 00:34:03.371
Um, you can create tab-delimited
t- uh, text files and export

00:34:03.372 --> 00:34:07.726
metadata into spreadsheet columns,
um, associated-- the, the existing

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:09.451
metadata that's already associated.

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:14.391
You can edit the alt text line by
line in Excel and then re-import

00:34:14.499 --> 00:34:18.911
and map those alt text accessibility
values back by file name.

00:34:19.143 --> 00:34:24.043
So you don't need to follow this
one-by-one approach of using Adobe Bridge.

00:34:24.279 --> 00:34:28.663
You can use an extension to help
manage this with a, with a spreadsheet.

00:34:29.621 --> 00:34:32.525
Next is ExifTool, and
it's a command line tool.

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:35.274
Um, it works off of standard CSV files.

00:34:35.521 --> 00:34:40.447
So you take a CSV with a source file
column, and you add an alt text field

00:34:40.447 --> 00:34:46.092
header, and then that just a simple
command applies each row to its image.

00:34:46.096 --> 00:34:49.746
So if you're more comfortable with
command line, um, you can use ExifTool.

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:54.043
Photo Mechanic, another great option.

00:34:54.290 --> 00:34:59.275
Um, so they also do tab-delimited
text or CSV files, so you can map

00:34:59.276 --> 00:35:01.917
file names or keys to text strings.

00:35:02.165 --> 00:35:05.517
Um, you can work with variable
placeholders like alt text to

00:35:05.518 --> 00:35:07.540
populate thousands of images at once.

00:35:07.715 --> 00:35:10.243
So another great option for bulk editing.

00:35:11.164 --> 00:35:14.960
And finally, Python, um, if you're
more comfortable with that, um, it

00:35:14.961 --> 00:35:22.944
combines ExifTool and PyExifTool, um,
working with CSV, XLSX or JSON files.

00:35:23.245 --> 00:35:28.607
Um, so a program like Pandas reads
rows ca- um, and calls ExifTool

00:35:28.630 --> 00:35:31.583
to embed each string of text.

00:35:31.820 --> 00:35:36.190
And then you can run automated checks,
flagging text over 150 characters,

00:35:36.403 --> 00:35:39.713
um, if you want to, if you're
checking for, for character count, um,

00:35:39.715 --> 00:35:41.685
before actually writing to the file.

00:35:41.696 --> 00:35:45.401
So there are extra capabilities
there, um, within Python.

00:35:48.091 --> 00:35:53.362
So to wrap up some of the key points
here, the key takeaways, when we

00:35:53.362 --> 00:35:58.332
connect the dots between embedded
metadata and WordPress 7.0, we are

00:35:58.332 --> 00:36:02.922
unlocking three major operational
wins for web accessibility.

00:36:03.253 --> 00:36:05.063
The first is efficiency gains.

00:36:05.288 --> 00:36:09.877
We eliminate blank slate manual
entry, saving content creators hours

00:36:09.878 --> 00:36:11.500
of tedious administrative work.

00:36:11.887 --> 00:36:16.464
By automatically pre-populating fields
on upload, we remove the primary point

00:36:16.465 --> 00:36:20.650
of friction that causes teams to skip
over alt text when deadlines hit.

00:36:21.383 --> 00:36:23.000
The second is data integrity.

00:36:23.326 --> 00:36:27.558
We shift from isolated database
text to portable metadata that

00:36:27.558 --> 00:36:30.143
protects human-authored descriptions.

00:36:30.606 --> 00:36:35.978
So, protecting that human quality, working
across collaborative workflows, um, this

00:36:35.978 --> 00:36:40.674
is another way to scale alt text in a
reliable way across your organization.

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:43.662
And then third is quality and context.

00:36:43.833 --> 00:36:47.364
We combine automated file reading
with the indispensable nuance

00:36:47.383 --> 00:36:49.950
of human review at publication.

00:36:50.389 --> 00:36:54.542
Automation gives us a strong
human-authored foundation, freeing up

00:36:54.544 --> 00:36:59.817
publishers to focus on-- focus all of
their energy on refining the text to

00:36:59.817 --> 00:37:04.091
fit the specific narrative of the page
rather than simply getting a description

00:37:04.102 --> 00:37:06.371
in any form in the alt attribute.

00:37:06.777 --> 00:37:13.184
So WordPress 7.0 is truly a massive leap
forward that allows us to build scalable,

00:37:13.233 --> 00:37:15.776
portable, and sustainable image workflows.

00:37:16.182 --> 00:37:19.038
So I just want to leave you
with some final thoughts today.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:25.139
Getting these fields into the IPTC
standard back in 2021 was step one.

00:37:25.592 --> 00:37:30.243
Getting WordPress Core to read
them in version 7.0 was step two.

00:37:30.778 --> 00:37:32.886
Step three is, of course, up to us.

00:37:33.383 --> 00:37:37.765
We need to build accessible, scalable
workflows for the entire web.

